WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.310
MARKUS KLUTE: Hello, everybody.

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:12.760
So this set of slides is on the
organization of this course.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:14.350
It's part of the syllabus.

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:17.530
I'll explain how the
course is structured,

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:20.710
how you participate,
how you'll be graded,

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:22.720
and I'll give you
some details and where

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.260
to find proper pointers.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:29.110
To get started,
this class will be

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:33.520
taught in an inverted classroom
or flipped classroom setting,

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:36.280
and it's going to be
online-only so we will not

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:40.970
have in-person
opportunities to discuss.

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:43.810
There's a second video
where I'll just briefly talk

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:46.810
about the strategy of
the inverted classroom

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:49.210
and the benefits.

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:52.030
What you are typically
used for as lectures

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:55.420
is organized solely
in short videos

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:58.000
where I discuss
concepts or methods.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.200
The videos also include
a few short questions

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.780
for your own
self-evaluation, and we'll

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:08.950
pick up those questions and
later in recitation session.

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:11.230
So when you meet
Tuesdays and Thursdays

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:14.110
we'll have time to
answer your questions.

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:18.520
We'll have time to go
through those [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:21.280
and hopefully have
a good discussion.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:25.525
The recitations are also used
for another concept, which

00:01:25.525 --> 00:01:27.340
I'll explain on a
later slide, which

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:30.700
is your presentation
of a specific paper.

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:34.210
This is part of
your homework set

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:37.300
is to find a paper to discuss
with me whether or not

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:40.030
it fits into this
class schedule,

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:44.630
and together with a partner,
have a short discussion of it.

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:47.390
So we'll use these Tuesday
and Thursday sessions

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:49.260
for this purpose.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:52.220
And in addition, there's going
to be an office hour on Friday.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:55.110
It's one hour where I'm just
going to be connected to a Zoom

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:55.610
meeting.

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:58.220
You can log in at
any time and we'll

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:01.640
discuss whatever you
want to talk about.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.950
For that let's contribute
to the Doodle poll, which

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:06.500
I posed during the
first [INAUDIBLE] time

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:09.380
of the first class,
and then we'll

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:12.220
find the best possible time
for everybody involved.

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:18.350
The course evaluation or your
evaluation in this course

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:20.020
will be made up 50%
out of homework.

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:24.970
So it's going to be six
PSets are going to be posted.

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:28.240
One will be forgiven so I'll
basically count five PSets,

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:34.300
and each will allow you
to accumulate 8 points.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:37.300
The paper presentation
I was talking about

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:39.460
will have 20 points.

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:42.700
We're talking about a 20-minute
presentation of a paper.

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:46.990
It's really a summary of
a paper of your choice

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:50.105
together with 10
minutes of Q&amp;A. Again,

00:02:50.105 --> 00:02:51.730
this is being done
in two groups so you

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:56.860
have to split up the aspect
of presenting-- preparing

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:00.160
and presenting,
and then also when

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.130
it comes to the response
and the questions.

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:06.130
And then there's going to
be two short oral exams.

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:07.990
This is 20 minutes,
so the actual exam

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:12.060
is only going to be 15 minutes,
with the teaching staff,

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:14.210
so this is Justin
and myself where

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:16.720
we ask you a couple
of questions and just

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:19.960
make sure that you are on top
of the content we discussed

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:21.862
in the weeks prior.

00:03:21.862 --> 00:03:22.570
So there is two.

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:25.180
The first one deals with the
particle physics content,

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:27.520
and the second one with
the nuclear physics

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:33.020
and experimental method as well.

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:35.920
The grading scheme
will not be worse

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:37.590
than what I've given you here.

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:43.550
The great divide-- the great
divide or the grade divide--

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:47.990
at 85% between A and
B, 70% between B and C,

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:51.165
60% between C and
D, and below 50%

00:03:51.165 --> 00:03:53.690
earns you an F. I
don't think anybody

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:58.570
can get into this environment
as long as they participate.

